AB  - Traditional, centralized security tools often struggle against adaptive, multi-vector attacks that evolve during an incident. We present the Multi-Agent LLM Cyber Defense Framework (MALCDF), a structured multi-agent architecture in which four large language model (LLM) agents—Detection, Intelligence, Response, and Analysis—coordinate through a Secure Communication Layer (SCL) with encrypted, ontologyaligned messaging. The framework emphasizes structured, audit-friendly outputs (e.g., MITRE ATT&CK mappings) and Security Operations Cente (SOC) aligned role separation. Evaluation is conducted under controlled conditions: all reported metrics are computed on the same 50-record live stream derived from the CICIDS2017 [1] feature schema. CICIDS2017 is used for configuration and to train a practical ML baseline. The ML-IDS baseline is a Lightweight Random Forest IDS (LRF-IDS) trained on a subset of CICIDS2017 and evaluated on the same 50-record stream, with no overlap between training and test records. On this controlled stream, MALCDF achieves 90.0% detection accuracy, an 85.7% F1-score, and a 9.1% falsepositive rate, with 6.8 s average per-event latency. Relative to the selected baselines, the architecture demonstrates improved detection accuracy while maintaining structured end-to-end reasoning outputs. Given the limited evaluation size, results should be interpreted as a controlled empirical study of coordinated LLM agents rather than large-scale statistical benchmarking.
ER  - 

