AB  - LLM powered autonomous agents do not have principled middleware for lifecycle management, orchestration of multi-agent systems, and risk-tailored cybersecurity. We introduce Agent OS - a five-layer operating system abstraction with an Agent Kernel, Zero-Trust Permission Controller, integrity-checked Memory Management, and an ephemerally-sandboxed Tiered Tool Registry designed to address the agentic cybersecurity domain. The newly introduced Orchestration Layer trains a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) policy with Contract-Based Compositional Shielding to avoid unsafe agent behaviors, circumventing the brittleness of hierarchical task planning while maintaining theoretical runtime safety guarantees. The Cybersecurity Integration Framework implements a novel approach to the MITRE ATT&CK for AI matrix, provisions Zero Trust Network Access between agents, and utilizes LoRA-adapted Small Language Models for zero-shot adversarial prompt detection with GRC-compliant audit logging capabilities for the EU AI Act and NIST AI RMF frameworks.Our initial implementation demonstrated on a proof-of-concept prototype across four use cases (two of which were adversarial redteam tests) achieved an 84.7% task success rate, reduced cybersecurity violations by 85.7%, decreased successful prompt injection attempts by 79.4% (from 68% to 11% of trials), and achieved a 12% reduction in latency versus uninstrumented baselines. We introduce the first formalism for a cybersecurity architecture in the domain of agentic AI systems and present a potential reference architecture for standardization within the community.
