AB  - Data validation has evolved from a static preprocessing step into a continuous requirement in modern AI-driven systems operating in high-volume and adversarial environments. This paper analyzes validation challenges across two domains: Security Operations Centers (SOC) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines. While traditional validation focuses on structural correctness and consistency, SOC and RAG systems require additional guarantees related to semantic integrity, temporal validity, and resilience against adversarial manipulation.Based on a structured analysis of recent approaches, we identify common validation patterns across these domains and highlight the increasing role of AI-assisted techniques, LLM-based reasoning, multi-agent validation, and reliability-aware retrieval. To support this analysis, we provide a comparative overview of existing validation methods, emphasizing their trade-offs in terms of real-time applicability and computational cost.Building on these observations, we outline a conceptual, agent-based validation architecture that integrates structural, semantic, and adversarial validation layers. A feasibility-oriented analysis shows that such systems are most practical as selective and hybrid pipelines, where high-cost LLM-based validation is applied only to high-risk cases. We conclude that data validation should be treated as a multi-layered architectural component essential for ensuring trust and robustness in both AI and security systems.
ER  - 

