AB  - Security Operations Centers (SOCs) face high alert volumes, fragmented telemetry, and limited analyst capacity. This paper presents AGSSA, an evidence-constrained agentic workflow for explainable SOC decision support. AGSSA combines modality-adaptive detection, feature-level explanation, short- and long-term memory, policy-controlled alert selection, and NIST SP 800-61r3/CSF 2.0-aligned reporting. The LLM is not used as a detector; instead, it converts structured detector outputs, explanation evidence, and memory records into auditable incident reports. AGSSA is evaluated across five datasets covering network-flow, IoT, Sysmon host-process, and heterogeneous host telemetry. On supervised datasets, AGSSA achieves 99.91% accuracy on CIC-IDS2017, 86.98% on UNSW-NB15, and 99.90% on TON_IoT. On MITRE BRAWL, AGSSA reduces 47,026 Sysmon events to 523 review candidates and validates them against BSF red-team ground truth. Results show that AGSSA is best understood not as a new IDS classifier, but as a memoryaware SOC triage workflow that reduces review volume while preserving explanation evidence, ATT&CK context, and report traceability.
ER  - 

