AB  - AI-equipped and integrated physical robots and drones are currently being used for an increasing number of security-related applications. This review examines recent work (from 2019-2024) on” embodied AI for security,” focusing on four main security-related applications - surveillance and patrol, border control, disaster response, and other associated domains - and compares the capabilities and autonomy of several types of representative embodiments (such as quadrupedal and wheeled robots, tethered drones). It identifies several vital challenges (related to hardware/computational constraints, real-world robustness, ethics/privacy, compatibility with existing systems, cybersecurity). It describes how they are being addressed by recent research trends (edge AI hardware, adversarial training, privacypreserving learning, standards/frameworks). Future directions for this technology (multi-agent coordination, foundation modeldriven planning, secure-by-design robotics) are described. Two tables provide summaries of the most notable security-related robotic systems and challenges, as well as emerging solutions.
