AB  - Sixth-generation (6G) networks introduce unprecedented complexity through terahertz communications, AI-native management, massive device deployments, and network slicing over virtualised infrastructure, expanding the attack surface well beyond what conventional correlation-based security methods can reliably address. Existing machine learning approaches to network security operate primarily at the associational level: they detect statistical anomalies but cannot support intervention-level policy evaluation or counterfactual incident forensics. This paper proposes causal reasoning as a principled methodology for 6G network security and presents the Causal Autonomy Framework (CAF) as a concrete implementation. CAF integrates large language model (LLM) generation with symbolic knowledge-base verification and structural causal model validation in a closed-loop architecture. We argue that the three levels of Pearl's causal hierarchy - association, intervention, and counterfactual reasoning - correspond directly to the three operational needs of a 6G security analyst: anomaly detection, security policy simulation, and incident forensics. We evaluate the framework on two benchmarks - a constructed 6G threat-scenario corpus (65 queries) and a scenario derived from the public 5G-NIDD intrusion dataset (40 queries) - against an LLM-only baseline, reporting confidence intervals and paired significance tests, and we analyse the computational complexity, latency, and deployment feasibility of the architecture in operational 6G environments.
ER  - 

