AB  - Network security administrators face a critical expertise gap: configuring intrusion detection systems and firewall policies requires specialized knowledge of both attack patterns and rule syntax. This paper investigates the efficacy of finetuned large language models in automating the generation of defensive security rules. We systematically evaluate baseline capabilities of instruction-tuned models across thirty distinct cyberattack scenarios, encompassing both traffic filtering and payload inspection rule sets. Three progressive fine-tuning strategies are implemented to enhance model comprehension of security semantics and rule syntax. Experimental results demonstrate substantial performance improvements, with fine-tuned models achieving 89% accuracy in generating syntactically correct and technically effective countermeasures, compared to 42% baseline performance. The study provides empirical evidence that domainspecific fine-tuning bridges the cybersecurity knowledge gap while maintaining rule efficiency and minimizing false positive potential.
