AB  - Accurately automating the analysis of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) reports to identify MITRE ATT&CK techniques remains a critical challenge due to the labor-intensive nature of manual mapping and the limitations of existing NLP and LLM-based methods, which often suffer from hallucinations, incoherent reasoning, and knowledge isolation. To address these challenges, we propose DebateCTI—a novel framework that integrates a multi-agent debate mechanism with parameter-efficient fine-tuning. Our approach employs role-specialized LLM agents that engage in structured, multi-round debates to explore diverse perspectives and collaboratively identify ATT&CK techniques. A lightweight fine-tuned judge module is further applied to filter out false positives and enhance reliability. Experimental evaluation on the TRAM dataset demonstrates that DebateCTI achieves an F1-score of 0.8479, which represents a relative improvement of 21.2% over the baseline. This work represents a successful application of multi-agent debate, providing an interpretable and scalable solution for CTI automated analysis that holds significant implications for real-world cyber defense systems. Our code is available at https://github.com/xjscode/DebateCTI.
ER  - 

